  "body": "### What problem are you trying to solve?\n\nI would like to strip EXIF data from file attachments before they ever reach a server. It would be nice the either the `<input type=\"file\">` or something in the DOM gave us a built in way to do this.\n\nObviously I could send it to a server and strip it there, then upload to S3, but thats horribly inefficient especially with things like direct uploads and pre-signed URLs making it even more challenging by making it a distributed problem.\n\n### What solutions exist today?\n\nVery complex manipulation that changes based on file type:\n\nhttps://stackoverflow.com/questions/27638402/strip-exif-data-from-image\n\nSome existing packages, but they are quite heavy with ones like `exifr` being >25kb minified + gzipped.\n\n### How would you solve it?\n\nI would like to either make an \"allowlist\" of minimal EXIF data to accept. things like `exifallow=\"orientation\"` for example so that we only store the minimal metadata we need, but don't need to store things like GPS coordinates. I imagine this could get large / cumbersome / awkward, so I don't know how useful it is at the attribute level.\n\nThe other option is something like a JS function to read the EXIF data for the given file type and be able to manually \"remove\" unnecessary data.\n\n### Anything else?\n\n_No response_",
